in late 2020 variants of the virus that  causes covid19 were discovered around  the world  first one in the uk then more in south  africa and  nigeria the question is what is their  significance  in public health terms government and  health officials  acted swiftly to try to contain the new  versions of the virus  we must therefore go into a national  lockdown which is tough enough  to contain this variant but what is a  variant and why  are they such a problem well let's start  with the basics  viruses are microscopic infectious  agents  and scientists debate whether they're  even alive that's because they can only  survive a limited time  outside a host cell once inside a cell  they take over its machinery and make  copies of themselves  over and over that's how they spread an  infection  so how does a virus mutate well  when it replicates random errors can  occur in its genetic  code that's the instructions for making  a new virus  these are called mutations and most  don't make  any difference but sometimes they give  the virus new properties  this is one of the reasons the flu  vaccine has to be updated  every year vaccines train the body's  immune system to fight the virus  but if the virus mutates and changes too  much a vaccine may no longer work so  what is a variant then well  when a virus mutates it becomes a  variant a slightly different version  if the mutation gives it a new  superpower like infecting more cells or  spreading in the air more easily  then it will make copies of itself  faster and become  the dominant version of the virus the  only way of detecting a new variant  is by taking samples from patients and  doing a readout of the virus's genetic  code  something the uk is very good at over  the last few days  thanks to our world-class genomic  capability in the uk  we have identified a new variant of  coronavirus  which may be associated with the faster  spread in the southeast of england  and likely why it was the first to  discover a significant variant  the concern about this particular  variant of covid19  is that it spreads more quickly our  scientists have confirmed  this new variant is between 50 and 70  percent  more transmissible that means you're  much much  more likely to catch the virus and to  pass it on  that led to a surge in cases starting in  kent before spreading across the uk and  beyond  the third national lockdown is an  attempt to bring the virus back  under control  [Music]  you
 

